jmk-wifi patch 0003: survive a surprise USB unplug

Why:
  On the car an unplug (or rmmod) while the interface is up can crash the kernel, and
  the head unit's kernel panics on an oops in interrupt context and on soft lockups
  (BOOTPARAM_SOFTLOCKUP_PANIC, PANIC_TIMEOUT=5), so the head unit reboots. Upstream's
  remove path has these problems:
  - rtw_dev_remove() unregisters the netdevs *before* it marks the device removed, so
    ndo_stop, cfg80211's stop_ap/disconnect and their commands run against a device
    that is gone: register I/O with retries and timeouts, H2C commands, frames, waits.
  - The rx and tx tasklets are never killed. A URB completion can schedule them just
    before the adapter is freed; they then run on freed memory in softirq context.
  - A tx URB completion always re-arms the tx tasklet, also after tx was stopped.
  - The vendor request loop keeps retrying after the device is known to be gone.
  - After the netdev is unregistered, the remove path can still call cfg80211
    (disconnected, del_sta, scan_done). A scan request still pending at NETDEV_DOWN is
    freed by cfg80211 while the driver keeps the pointer.

What changes (all guards use the driver's own bSurpriseRemoved / bDriverStopped):
  usb_intf.c
    rtw_dev_remove(): if the usb core has marked the device NOTATTACHED, set
      surprise-removed before anything else; skip LeaveAllPowerSaveMode() then.
    usb_intf_stop(): after the URBs are killed, tasklet_kill() the rx and tx tasklets.
  usb_ops_linux.c
    usbctrl_vendorreq(): stop retrying once RTW_CANNOT_IO; skip the trailing 0x4e0
      write then.
    usb_write_port_complete(): do not schedule the tx tasklet when RTW_CANNOT_TX.
  xmit_linux.c
    rtw_os_xmit_schedule(): same rule.
  rtw_cmd.c
    rtw_cmd_filter(): refuse new commands once the device is removed, so callers
      waiting for an ack (RTW_CMDF_WAIT_ACK) return at once.
  os_intfs.c
    netdev_close(), netdev_vir_if_close(): after the scan-abort waits, complete any
      scan request the driver still holds, before cfg80211's NETDEV_DOWN frees it.
    rtw_dev_unload(): no IPS register probe on a removed device.
  ioctl_cfg80211.c
    cfg80211_rtw_stop_ap(): nothing to do in hardware once removed (the AP state is
      freed by free_mlme_ap_info() on the remove path).
    rtw_cfg80211_indicate_scan_done(), _indicate_disconnect(), _indicate_sta_disassoc():
      do not call into cfg80211 once the netdev is unregistered (adapter->registered).
  rtw_ap.c
    ap_free_sta(): no delba/deauth frames when RTW_CANNOT_RUN.

Not changed: the netdev keeps the USB interface as its sysfs parent (SET_NETDEV_DEV).
Both netdevs and the wiphy are unregistered synchronously inside the disconnect
callback, which device_del() of the interface calls before it removes the interface's
own kobject, so there is no kobject removal conflict. See README.md.

